==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Unclear origin and meaning; there are several theories. The name probably comes from one of the many Nahuatl languages ([[w:Uto-Aztecan languages|Uto-Aztecan]] language family): (1) One claim says that it comes from {{etyl|nah|en}} {{m|nah|amoxco||Place of Books}}; (2) another is that it began as {{m|nah|amuchco||where there is tin}}, composed of {{m|nah|amuchitl||[[tin]]}} and {{m|nah|-co||in, at}}; (3) a third idea is that it comes from {{etyl|nah|en}} {{m|nah|amochitl||where there is [[copper]]}}.

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# An indigenous language of Mexico ([[w:Oto-Manguean languages|Oto-Manguean language family]]) with about 30,000 speakers, mostly in Oaxaca state.

